# 10. PettingZoo should define our simulator interface

I now think **PettingZoo is more important than Mesa long-term**.

PettingZoo is explicitly an API standard for multi-agent environments, with sequential and parallel interaction interfaces. ([GitHub][13])

So define the prehistoric world as:

```python
env = PaleoArena(
    arena=arena,
    weather=weather,
    animals=animals
)

observations = env.reset(seed=...)
actions = policies(observations)
state = env.step(actions)
```

Even if the first policies are handwritten.

Later:

```text
utility AI
↓
Monte Carlo planning
↓
reinforcement learning
```

can all use the same environment interface.

That is excellent architecture.

---

# 11. Mesa is still useful

Mesa is a mature Python agent-based modeling framework with agent management, spatial models, browser visualization and built-in data collection. ([Mesa][14])

Its documentation even includes the classic Wolf-Sheep predator/prey model. ([Mesa][14])

I would use Mesa for **rapid exploration**, not make it an irreversible dependency.

Something like:

```text
v0 research prototype:
Mesa

canonical simulator interface:
PettingZoo-compatible

high-performance kernel later:
custom NumPy / Rust / C++
```

