# 12. Physics: Pymunk now, PyBullet later

For your VPS, I would start **2D continuous**, not 3D.

Pymunk gives Python-accessible 2D rigid-body physics including collisions and impulse solving. ([Pymunk][15])

That gives us:

```text
mass
momentum
collision shapes
contact
velocity
terrain obstacles
turning
```

without running Unity.

The creature is not literally modeled as a perfect snake skeleton yet.

Instead:

```text
T. rex
≈ weighted oriented body regions

Titanoboa
≈ articulated/segmented chain
```

Later, if physical realism becomes worth the complexity, PyBullet gives a proper 3D rigid-body simulation foundation. Bullet is open source and exposes collision and rigid-body dynamics; PyBullet is commonly used for robotics and RL simulation. ([PyBullet][16])

---

# 13. Behavior shouldn't initially be RL

I'd start with **utility AI**.

Every animal considers actions:

```text
APPROACH
RETREAT
CIRCLE
CHASE
AMBUSH
DEFEND
REPOSITION
REST
```

Each gets a score:

```text
utility(attack) =
    confidence
  × positional_advantage
  × target_vulnerability
  × stamina
  × behavioral_prior
```

Then choose probabilistically.

That lets uncertainty propagate naturally.

Later, PettingZoo means we can train policies without rewriting the environment. ([PettingZoo][17])

